---
// The text of the sample concept note, as the rendering slice will write it.
// A link to a note that is not published is plain text (REN-04).
---

<div class='note-text'>
    <h2 id='idea'>The idea</h2>
    <p>
        The Zettelkasten method treats a collection of notes as a partner to think with. Each note
        is small, written in your own words, and linked to the notes it relates to. Over the years,
        the links matter more than any single note: they show paths between ideas that you did not
        plan.
    </p>
    <h2 id='cards'>Small cards, one idea each</h2>
    <p>
        A card holds one idea, so that a link to it means one thing. This is what{' '}
        <a class='link' href='#'>
            atomic notes
        </a>{' '}
        are about. A long note that mixes five ideas is hard to link, because a link to it says
        almost nothing.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
        <p>A note is worth what it connects to.</p>
    </blockquote>
    <h2 id='links'>Links over folders</h2>
    <p>
        Folders force each note into one place. Links let a note live in many contexts at once. I
        keep a few folders for the type of a note, and let the links carry the meaning.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Link a new note to at least one older note.</li>
        <li>Write why the link exists, in one sentence next to it.</li>
        <li>
            Collect related notes in a map of content, such as{' '}
            <span class='not-published'>My reading backlog</span>, when a topic grows.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <h2 id='practice'>How I practice it</h2>
    <p>
        I capture fast in the daily note, and I write the permanent notes later, when I can say the
        idea clearly. In Obsidian, a note type and a template keep the structure the same. The
        shortcut
        <code>Ctrl+O</code> finds any note in a second.
    </p>
    <h2 id='limits'>Limits</h2>
    <p>
        The method takes time before it pays back. With fewer than a few hundred notes, the links
        are thin, and the system feels like extra work. It is a long game.
    </p>
</div>
